{
  "part": 28,
  "addendum": "non-fusion learned baselines",
  "commit": "886dda3",
  "sklearn_version": "1.9.1",
  "label_meaning": "A window is attack=1 if ANY officially labelled line falls in it, and on both scenarios that label is dominated by one continuously-running DNS exfiltration tool (1,026 of 1,031 attack windows on russellmitchell and 663 of 667 on santos carry only dnsteal-family steps, docs/part26-ait-loader.md sections 0 and 12), so the task is 'was the exfiltration tool running in this window', NOT 'detect an APT' and not 'detect a multi-step intrusion'.",
  "determinism": "Logistic regression with the lbfgs solver is a deterministic function of its inputs: no initialisation draw, no minibatch order, so a seed has nothing to select and five runs return five identical numbers. Each baseline row is therefore ONE value with no +/-. Reporting +/- 0.0000 over five identical runs would fake the 5-SEED RULE rather than satisfy it. Determinism is checked, not assumed: every model is refitted and its coefficients and predicted probabilities asserted bit-identical. The naive and PrismFlow reference rows DO carry a +/-, because those are 5-seed means over model initialisation and batch order; the two kinds of row are not the same kind of estimate.",
  "pooling_policy": "Direction A and direction B are reported separately and are never pooled, averaged or concatenated.",
  "solver": {
    "solver": "lbfgs",
    "max_iter": 5000,
    "C": 1.0,
    "class_weight": null
  },
